    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assertion $(1)$ is a consequence of Lemma <a href="/tag/96ECD5" data-tag="96ECD5">5.5.5.14</a>, which we will prove in §<a href="/tag/1B8C35" data-tag="1B8C35">5.5.5</a>. The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (3)$ follows immediately from Proposition <a href="/tag/A4E3E0" data-tag="A4E3E0">5.2.7.8</a>. We now prove $(4)$. Lemma <a href="/tag/C7F127" data-tag="C7F127">5.5.4.11</a> implies that the collection of $S$-equivalences is a strongly saturated class of morphisms containing $S$; it therefore contains $\overline{S}$, so that $(ii) \Rightarrow (i)$. Now suppose that $f: X \rightarrow Y$ is such that $Lf$ is an equivalence, and consider the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { X \ar [r] \ar [dr] \ar [d] &amp;  Y \ar [d] \\ LX \ar [r] &amp;  LY} \]
</div>
    <p>
       Our proof of $(1)$ shows that the vertical morphisms belong to $\overline{S}$, and the lower horizontal arrow belongs to $\overline{S}$ by Remark <a href="/tag/4E69BB" data-tag="4E69BB">5.5.4.6</a>. Two applications of the two-out-of-three property now show that $f \in \overline{S}$, so that $(iii) \Rightarrow (ii)$. If $f$ is an $S$-equivalence, then we may again use the above diagram and the two-out-of-three property to conclude that $Lf$ is an equivalence. It follows that $LX$ and $LY$ co-represent the same functor on the homotopy category $h\calC '$, so that Yoneda's lemma implies that $Lf$ is an equivalence. Thus $(i) \Rightarrow (iii)$ and the proof of $(4)$ is complete. 
    <p>
      It remains to prove $(2)$. Remark <a href="/tag/BCB411" data-tag="BCB411">5.2.7.5</a> implies that $L \calC $ admits small colimits, so it will suffice to prove that $L \calC $ is accessible. According to Proposition <a href="/tag/BC92F6" data-tag="BC92F6">5.5.4.2</a>, this follows from the implication $(iii) \Rightarrow (i)$ of assertion $(4)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
